<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.1.4: Explain Why an Expression Is a Perfect Square</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02339</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.1.4: Explain Why an Expression Is a Perfect Square</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4dde933a-ca3f-4fbf-9f7c-44806a52c4c5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Explain why it makes sense to call the expression \(x^2+20x+100\) a <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">perfect square</term>.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>It is equivalent to \((x+10)^2\), so it is a factor squared.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Solve \(x^2+20x+100=81\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solutions to this equation. </para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(x = -1\) and \(x = -19\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
